WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:01.850
With a fixed rate,

00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:06.690
later noisy batches can keep moving
parameters around the training optimum.

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:09.610
A decreasing rate may settle the fit.

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:14.090
We test that question while holding SGD,
batch thirty two,

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:16.490
initial rate zero point zero one,

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:19.550
paired orders and ten passes fixed.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:22.620
There is one declared
inverse time schedule,

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:26.630
rather than a search across many
shapes after looking at validation.

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:32.750
Its progress measure is examples already
processed before the next batch update.

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:34.910
That timing is part of the rule.

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:40.130
We will compare both training squared
error and later absolute error.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:44.840
Better optimization of the first quantity
does not mathematically guarantee an

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:46.150
improvement in the second.

00:00:48.275 --> 00:00:53.558
The base rate is divided by one plus
examples seen over forty three thousand,

00:00:53.558 --> 00:00:54.825
two hundred and twenty five.

00:00:56.155 --> 00:00:59.485
That denominator equals five
complete training passes.

00:01:00.355 --> 00:01:03.705
At zero examples,
the rate is zero point zero one.

00:01:04.195 --> 00:01:09.285
At five passes, the denominator is two,
giving zero point zero zero five.

00:01:10.335 --> 00:01:12.565
At ten passes it is three,

00:01:12.735 --> 00:01:17.925
so the next rate would be about zero
point zero zero three three three three.

00:01:18.435 --> 00:01:21.425
The run has already reached
its work budget then.

00:01:22.095 --> 00:01:25.745
A saved next rate describes
what a future update would use;

00:01:25.935 --> 00:01:28.445
it does not imply an
extra update happened.

00:01:28.935 --> 00:01:33.055
Writing the progress unit explicitly
prevents confusion between parameter

00:01:33.055 --> 00:01:35.165
updates and examples processed.

00:01:37.317 --> 00:01:40.447
First calculate the rate
from completed exposure.

00:01:41.297 --> 00:01:44.927
Then obtain the next batch and
compute its actual mean gradient.

00:01:45.517 --> 00:01:48.587
Finally apply the optimizer
step with that rate.

00:01:48.997 --> 00:01:53.047
If we advanced the example counter
before evaluating the schedule,

00:01:53.377 --> 00:01:56.747
even the first batch would start
below the declared base rate.

00:01:57.237 --> 00:02:00.787
At the tail,
count the five examples actually visited,

00:02:01.077 --> 00:02:03.087
not thirty two imaginary ones.

00:02:03.637 --> 00:02:08.152
One hundred updates means one
hundred examples for single rows,

00:02:08.152 --> 00:02:10.347
thirty two hundred for
ordinary mini batches,

00:02:10.857 --> 00:02:14.887
or eight hundred and sixty four thousand,
five hundred for full batches.

00:02:15.857 --> 00:02:20.167
Choosing examples as the clock makes
the intended exposure meaning explicit.

00:02:20.737 --> 00:02:23.107
Choosing updates could
also define a study,

00:02:23.257 --> 00:02:25.347
but it would be a different
schedule contract.

00:02:27.525 --> 00:02:31.015
The decayed rule gets closer
to the training optimum.

00:02:31.745 --> 00:02:34.832
Mean half squared error falls
from about three thousand,

00:02:34.832 --> 00:02:38.645
one hundred and seventy seven
point nine zero to three thousand,

00:02:38.645 --> 00:02:40.995
one hundred and sixty
eight point three five.

00:02:41.725 --> 00:02:45.052
The earlier converged reference
is about three thousand,

00:02:45.052 --> 00:02:47.635
one hundred and sixty
seven point five three.

00:02:48.605 --> 00:02:53.445
Yet mean validation absolute error
increases from one hundred and sixteen

00:02:53.445 --> 00:02:57.575
point one seven to one hundred
and sixteen point five six.

00:02:58.125 --> 00:03:00.875
That is about zero point three nine worse.

00:03:01.905 --> 00:03:03.655
Seed spread is smaller with decay,

00:03:03.945 --> 00:03:06.595
but that alone does not
make the forecast better.

00:03:07.125 --> 00:03:07.955
The two objectives,

00:03:08.365 --> 00:03:13.015
their data windows and their units must
stay distinct when reading the result.

00:03:15.377 --> 00:03:19.877
Pairing shows that decay worsens
validation absolute error for seeds

00:03:19.877 --> 00:03:24.327
seventeen and forty three,
and slightly improves it for twenty nine.

00:03:25.037 --> 00:03:27.227
We keep that detail beside the mean.

00:03:27.817 --> 00:03:31.957
A quieter final trajectory therefore
supports a claim about optimization

00:03:31.957 --> 00:03:36.987
stability in this run, rather than
universal forecast superiority.

00:03:38.097 --> 00:03:41.087
All trials stop after
their example allowance.

00:03:41.637 --> 00:03:45.947
We did not configure a convergence test
or stop because a curve looked flat.

00:03:46.417 --> 00:03:47.907
Time, update,

00:03:48.497 --> 00:03:52.147
divergence and nonfinite stops
have separate recorded reasons.

00:03:52.997 --> 00:03:57.567
Changing the decay interval after reading
validation would be a new comparison.

00:03:58.357 --> 00:03:59.367
For continuation,

00:03:59.877 --> 00:04:04.577
save the example count and schedule
configuration so the next rate follows the

00:04:04.577 --> 00:04:07.687
same clock instead of
restarting at the base value.

